  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>When an apartment building or group of units is declared a total loss and the insurance offer will not cover rebuilding, appraisal for total loss claims can be a way to settle the dispute over the amount without going to court. Most property policies include an appraisal clause that lets either side request it. Understanding how it works, and when outside help makes sense, can mean the difference between rebuilding properly and absorbing a large loss.</p>

<h2>What "total loss" means for apartment units</h2>
<p>A total loss usually means the property is destroyed, or the cost to repair it exceeds a threshold set by the policy or the insurer, such as its value or the coverage limit. For apartment buildings, it often follows a fire, tornado, hurricane or severe water damage that leaves the structure unsafe.</p>
<p>How a total loss is paid depends on your policy:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Replacement cost</strong> coverage pays to rebuild with materials of like kind and quality, often after you actually rebuild.</li>
<li><strong>Actual cash value</strong> coverage pays replacement cost minus depreciation.</li>
<li><strong>Policy limits</strong> cap what is paid, which is why coverage that keeps up with construction costs matters.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many commercial and landlord policies also include coverage for lost rents and code upgrades (often called ordinance or law coverage). If your building has to be rebuilt to current code, those costs can be significant.</p>

<h2>How the appraisal clause works</h2>
<p>Policy wording varies, but appraisal typically follows this path:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A disagreement on amount.</strong> You and the insurer agree damage is covered but disagree on how much it costs to repair or rebuild.</li>
<li><strong>A written demand.</strong> Either side requests appraisal in writing, following the policy's steps.</li>
<li><strong>Each side picks an appraiser.</strong> Policies often require them to be competent and disinterested.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers select an umpire.</strong> If they cannot agree, a court may appoint one.</li>
<li><strong>Each appraiser values the loss.</strong> They inspect, review estimates and try to agree.</li>
<li><strong>Agreement by any two sets the amount.</strong> If the appraisers do not agree, the umpire decides on the differences, and an award signed by two of the three is usually binding on the amount.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each side typically pays its own appraiser and splits the umpire's cost. Appraisal generally resolves the amount of loss, not coverage questions. If the insurer denies that something is covered at all, that is a different kind of dispute.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Before invoking appraisal, get detailed, line-item rebuild estimates from licensed contractors who know multifamily construction. A vague number is hard to defend; a detailed estimate gives your appraiser something solid to work with.</p></div>

<h2>When to consider appraisal or outside help</h2>
<p>Appraisal can make sense when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer's estimate is far below contractor quotes</li>
<li>Repeated negotiation has stalled</li>
<li>The disagreement is about scope, pricing or the amount of damage rather than whether it is covered</li>
</ul>
<p>Many owners bring in a licensed public adjuster before or during this process. A public adjuster represents the policyholder, documents the full loss and negotiates with the insurer. In Texas, public adjusters must be licensed by the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/">Texas Department of Insurance</a>, and you can verify a license on the TDI website. Firms such as <a href="https://txpublicadjusting.com/">Texas Public Adjusting</a> work on multifamily and total loss claims and can explain whether appraisal is likely to help in your situation.</p>
<p>If the dispute involves coverage, bad faith or deadlines under the Texas Insurance Code, talk with an attorney. This article is general information, not legal or insurance advice.</p>

<h2>Documenting the loss</h2>
<p>Whether you negotiate, use a public adjuster or go to appraisal, documentation drives the outcome:</p>
<ul>
<li>Photos and video of every unit, common area and exterior, before cleanup if safe</li>
<li>Pre-loss condition records, rent rolls and leases</li>
<li>Building plans, permits and records of past renovations</li>
<li>Contractor estimates and engineering reports</li>
<li>Receipts for emergency repairs and mitigation</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep originals protected and organized from the start.</p>
<!--products:fireproof_safe,document_organizer,laser_measure,moisture_meter|Tools for documenting a large property loss-->
<p>A [[amz:laser_measure|laser measure]] helps you record room dimensions quickly for estimates, and a [[amz:moisture_meter|moisture meter]] documents how far water has spread in partially standing units.</p>

<h2>Disaster aid and tenants</h2>
<p>After a federally declared disaster, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/">FEMA</a> may offer assistance to individuals, and the Small Business Administration may offer disaster loans to property owners. Programs and eligibility change, so check FEMA's site directly after an event.</p>
<p>Tenants in a destroyed building face their own questions about leases, deposits and temporary housing. Under the Texas Property Code, when a casualty makes a unit totally or partially unusable, leases can often be terminated or rent adjusted. Owners should communicate early and follow the lease and law. Renters can read our guide on <a href="/can-you-stay-in-your-apartment-during-repairs-what-renters-need-to-know/">staying in an apartment during repairs</a>.</p>

<h2>Planning the rebuild</h2>
<p>Once you know the settlement amount, decide whether to rebuild as before, rebuild with upgrades, or sell the land. Things to consider:</p>
<ul>
<li>Current code requirements, which may add cost. See <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">navigating Texas building codes</a>.</li>
<li>Insurance requirements for collecting replacement cost holdbacks, which often require proof of rebuilding</li>
<li>Rebuild timeline and how long rents will be lost</li>
<li>Opportunities to improve resilience, such as better roofing or drainage</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep in mind that insurance policies often set deadlines for completing repairs to collect full replacement cost, so read your policy and ask the insurer in writing if you need more time. Our <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">construction timeline guide</a> gives a sense of how long each phase can take, though multifamily projects usually run longer than single-family homes.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
